href: https://xwiki.ewi.tudelft.nl/xwiki/rest/wikis/sce2026group04/spaces/tmp%3A%20Pitch/spaces/1%20Foundation/spaces/a3%20Problem%20Scenario/spaces/a3.1%20Daily%20Walk/pages/WebHome
title: a3.1 Daily Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** The daily walk breaks down at three recurring moments. Mid-walk, the resident loses track of where they are and why, and turns back or heads for their former home (PS-01). Before the walk, the resident sees no purpose in going out and has no one to go with (PS-02). In bad weather, the walk is replaced by indoor exercise that fails to hold the resident's attention (PS-03). In all three, the care worker's limited time is a contributing cause.

= Problem Scenario - Losing the thread mid-walk [PS-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-01
|Name|Losing the thread mid-walk
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-01, SA-02 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or care worker has confirmed it.

* Lai & Arthur (2003): wandering behaviour in people with dementia
)))
|Narrative|(((
After lunch, a care worker takes three residents on the garden circuit. One of them walks with a rollator. Halfway round, another resident needs the toilet, and the care worker steps away. The resident with the rollator stops at a bench and looks around. They no longer know where they are or why they are outside. They ask to go back to their room, and the walk ends after five minutes.

A week later the same moment goes differently. The resident walks to the garden gate, saying they must go home and naming the street where they lived twenty years ago. The care worker catches up at the gate and talks them back inside.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-02: Care worker
)))
|Activities involved|(((
* SA-01: Accompanied daily walk
* SA-02: Fitting walks between care tasks
)))
|Breakdown point|Companion's attention lapses → resident loses place and purpose of the walk
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Impaired spatial orientation and short-term memory
* Companion is the resident's only anchor to place and purpose
* Long-term memory of the former home outweighs the present

Contributing causes:

* One care worker for several residents
* Late-afternoon restlessness (sundowning)
* Fatigue
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Orientation: resident no longer knows where they are
1. Purpose: resident no longer knows why they are walking
1. Continuation: resident turns back instead of finishing the walk
1. Exit-seeking: resident sets out for the former home
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Physically stopping or restraining the resident - stays with ST-02
* Locking or securing exits - ST-05 policy
* Tracking whereabouts outside the walk - general surveillance
* Routes beyond the garden - garden by default; wider routes only where the resident's needs and abilities allow (ST-06 assessment)
)))

== Graph edges ==

illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-02, SA-01, SA-02.

= Problem Scenario - No reason to go out [PS-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-02
|Name|No reason to go out
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-01, SA-02, SA-04 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or care worker has confirmed it.

* Lyketsos et al. (2002): apathy is among the most common neuropsychiatric symptoms in dementia
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes
)))
|Narrative|(((
At home, the resident walked their dog twice a day. In the care home there is no dog and no errand to run. A care worker suggests a walk, and the resident answers: "What for?" They spend the afternoon in their chair.

Two doors down lives another resident who also sits alone most afternoons. Both enjoy talking about the harbour where they once worked. Nobody has had the time to bring them together. A volunteer comes once a week. Between visits the resident rarely goes out, and over the weeks they become less steady on their feet and lower in mood.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-02: Care worker
* ST-04: Walking partner (potential)
* ST-07: Volunteer
)))
|Activities involved|(((
* SA-01: Accompanied daily walk
* SA-02: Fitting walks between care tasks
* SA-04: Walking with a fellow resident
)))
|Breakdown point|Resident declines the walk: no purpose, no company
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Apathy, a common symptom of dementia
* Loss of the routines that gave walking a purpose (dog, errands)
* Loneliness

Contributing causes:

* No staff time to motivate one-to-one
* No overview of which residents would get along
* Volunteers available only occasionally
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Initiation: resident sees no reason to start
1. Company: no partner available at the right time
1. Matching: suitable partner unknown to staff
1. Engagement: walk is dull → resident turns back early
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Treating depression or apathy - ST-06
* Persuading a resident who clearly declines - resident's own choice
* Pairing residents without the consent of both
)))

== Graph edges ==

illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-02, ST-04, ST-07, SA-01, SA-02, SA-04.

= Problem Scenario - Rain cancels the walk [PS-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|PS-03
|Name|Rain cancels the walk
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Constructed from SA-01, SA-02, SA-03 and the a2.1 stakeholder records; literature and group reasoning only. No resident or care worker has confirmed it.

* Forbes et al. (2015): exercise may improve ADL ability in dementia
* Anderson-Hanley et al. (2012): cycling with a virtual route holds attention better than plain cycling (older adults, not dementia-specific)
)))
|Narrative|(((
It rains for a week in November, and the daily walks are cancelled. The resident uses a wheelchair. A care worker wheels them to the pedal exerciser in the common room and starts it. After three minutes the resident stops pedalling and asks where they are going. The care worker is called away, and the exerciser is not used again that week. In the evenings the resident is restless.
)))
|Stakeholders involved|(((
* ST-01: Resident with dementia
* ST-02: Care worker
* ST-06: Physiotherapist
)))
|Activities involved|(((
* SA-01: Accompanied daily walk
* SA-02: Fitting walks between care tasks
* SA-03: Bad-weather indoor exercise
)))
|Breakdown point|Indoor exercise without destination or company does not hold the resident's attention
|Causes|(((
Root causes:

* Exercise without destination: no route, nothing to see
* The walk's company and conversation are missing

Contributing causes:

* Staff needed to set up and start each session
* Shared device, occupied or unfamiliar
* Short, dark winter days limit outdoor walks further
)))
|Sub-problems|(((
1. Substitution: nothing replaces the cancelled walk
1. Engagement: resident stops after a few minutes
1. Set-up: each session needs a care worker to start it
1. Accessibility: device must be usable from a wheelchair
)))
|Out of scope|(((
* Prescribing exercise intensity - ST-06
* Walking outside in unsafe weather (ice, storm)
* Building changes such as a covered walkway - ST-05
)))

== Graph edges ==

illustrates → Problem; involves → ST-01, ST-02, ST-06, SA-01, SA-02, SA-03.

= References =

* Anderson-Hanley, C. et al. (2012). Exergaming and older adult cognition: a cluster randomized clinical trial. //American Journal of Preventive Medicine//, 42(2), 109-119.
* Forbes, D. et al. (2015). Exercise programs for people with dementia. //Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews//, (4), CD006489.
* Lai, C. K. Y. & Arthur, D. G. (2003). Wandering behaviour in people with dementia. //Journal of Advanced Nursing//, 44(2), 173-182.
* Lyketsos, C. G. et al. (2002). Prevalence of neuropsychiatric symptoms in dementia and mild cognitive impairment: results from the Cardiovascular Health Study. //JAMA//, 288(12), 1475-1483.
* Victor, C. R. (2012). Loneliness in care homes: a neglected area of research? //Aging Health//, 8(6), 637-646.
